<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parmigianino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1503">1503</a>–<a href="/years/1540">1540</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Girolamo Francesco Maria Mazzola</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parmigianino, born Girolamo Francesco Maria Mazzola in Parma, was a painter and printmaker of the Mannerist style. After working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> he returned to Parma, where he painted frescoes in the church of Santa Maria della Steccata. His works, including the Madonna with the Long Neck and the Self-portrait in a Convex Mirror, are known for their elongated, elegant figures.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parmigianino.jpg" alt="Parmigianino" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Parmigianino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parmigianino_Selfportrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Parmigianino, meaning the little one from Parma, was the eighth child of Filippo Mazzola and Donatella Abbati. His father died of the plague two years after his birth, and the children were raised by their uncles Michele and Pier Ilario, who according to Vasari were modestly talented artists. He completed a chapel decoration in San Giovanni Evangelista begun by his uncle and had finished the Bardi Altarpiece by the age of eighteen. In 1521 he was sent to Viadana to escape the wars between the French, Imperial and papal armies. There he painted two tempera panels and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-da-correggio">Correggio</a>, who was at work on the fresco decoration of the cupola.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1524 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with five small paintings, among them the Circumcision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and his Self-portrait in a Convex Mirror, hoping for the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a>. Vasari records that he was hailed there as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> reborn. In January 1526 he agreed to paint the Vision of Saint Jerome for the church of San Salvatore in Lauro, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527 drove him and many other artists out of the city. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> for nearly three years and was back in Parma by 1530.</p><p>In 1531 he was commissioned to paint two altarpieces for the unfinished church of Santa Maria della Steccata. The work was still unfinished in 1535. The Madonna with the Long Neck, which he began in 1534, was meant for the chapel of the family of Elena Baiardi. Parmigianino was a pioneer of Italian etching and designed chiaroscuro woodcuts that other artists cut. He made his etchings and chiaroscuro prints between 1527 and 1530 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, and although his output was small, it had a considerable influence on Italian printmaking. He also led a trend towards three-quarter and full-length portraits, previously mostly reserved for royalty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Around this time he became a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a>. Scholars now think his scientific interests may have come from a search for a new medium for his etchings. He did little work in the church, and the confraternity banned him from continuing. He was imprisoned for two months for breach of contract and was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giulio-romano">Giulio Romano</a>, who also withdrew.</p><p>He died of a fever in Casalmaggiore on 24 August 1540, aged 37, and was buried in the church of the Servite Friars. His cousin Girolamo Bedoli and Bedoli's son Alessandro Mazzola were among those he influenced. His drawings are outstanding, and his portable works are now in major museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and elsewhere.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parmigianino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Parma also inherited a great artistic legacy from the early sixteenth century, the dome frescoes of Correggio and the works of <b>Parmigianino</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Parma, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Casalmaggiore, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.8,10.33&amp;year=1527" aria-label="Parma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4796 581 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Parmigianino"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5246 851h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5246 851h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.8,10.33&amp;year=1527">What was here in 1527? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
